; ============================================================
; TITLE:    INSPI Designs
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     turtle geometry, recursion, symmetry, measuring
; SUMMARY:  INSPI from Turtle Geometry keeps the side fixed but grows the angle, and draws surprising symmetric designs.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Draws four INSPI designs, one in each quarter of the screen. Each
;   design is measured first with the pen up, then scaled and moved so
;   that it fits neatly in its own box.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. To draw one by hand try  clean inspi 5 2 7 360  (side,
;   first angle, how much the angle grows, and how many steps).
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * changing an input a little on every step
;   * a "dry run" with the pen up to measure a drawing
;   * scaling a drawing to fit a box
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Use an increment that divides 360, like 10 or 20, and count arms.
;   * Try increments of 7, 11 and 13 (they share no factor with 360).
;   * Change the side: does the shape change or only its size?
; ============================================================

; In Turtle Geometry (Abelson and diSessa, 1981) INSPI comes right after
; POLY. POLY always turns by the same angle; INSPI adds a little to the
; angle every time:
;     TO INSPI :SIDE :ANGLE :INC
;       FORWARD :SIDE  RIGHT :ANGLE
;       INSPI :SIDE :ANGLE + :INC :INC
;     END
; The book's version runs forever. Ours takes a number of steps, so it
; can stop and let the next design have a turn.

to inspi :side :angle :inc :steps
  repeat :steps [
    forward :side
    right :angle
    make "angle :angle + :inc
  ]
end

; MEASURE walks the whole design with the pen up and remembers the
; smallest and biggest x and y it reaches. It outputs [left right bottom top].
to measure :angle :inc :steps
  localmake "box (list 0 0 0 0)
  penup home
  repeat :steps [
    forward 1
    right :angle
    make "angle :angle + :inc
    make "box (list min xcor first :box  max xcor item 2 :box ~
                    min ycor item 3 :box  max ycor last :box)
  ]
  output :box
end

to min :a :b
  output ifelse :a < :b [:a] [:b]
end

to max :a :b
  output ifelse :a > :b [:a] [:b]
end

; DESIGN measures with a side of 1, works out how big a side fits in a
; box of the given size, then starts the turtle so the design is centred.
to design :cx :cy :angle :inc :steps :color
  localmake "b measure :angle :inc :steps
  localmake "w (item 2 :b) - (item 1 :b)
  localmake "h (item 4 :b) - (item 3 :b)
  localmake "side 240 / (max :w :h)
  ; the middle of the measured box, in side-1 units
  localmake "mx ((item 1 :b) + (item 2 :b)) / 2
  localmake "my ((item 3 :b) + (item 4 :b)) / 2
  penup
  setxy :cx - :mx * :side :cy - :my * :side
  setheading 0
  setpencolor :color
  pendown
  inspi :side :angle :inc :steps
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [20 24 40]
setpensize 2
design -200 145 2 7 720 [255 200 60]
design 200 145 5 40 72 [90 200 255]
design -200 -145 2 20 90 [255 110 150]
design 200 -145 3 13 720 [140 230 120]
